msgid "Attach documents and media to a unit"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Tie photographs, drawings and models to the units they show, and gather "
"them in groups and documents.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A unit is described by the pictures that show it: the photographs of a "
"campaign, a drawing, a detail, a model. EMStudio links them to the unit "
"without copying them: a file stays where it is on the disk, and the graph"
" records which unit it shows. The **Inspector** of every node opens with "
"a strip of its media, and from the strip a viewer of the images lets you "
"sort them, group them and make documents of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "Before you start"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Open a study (the copy of Templu Mare from :doc:`/tutorial/first-study` "
"is fine). The **Sources** workspace is a good place: the **Outliner** "
"lists the units and the documents, and the **Inspector** shows the strip."
msgstr ""

msgid "The pictures must be in a folder that EMStudio can read."
msgstr ""

msgid "Link pictures to a unit"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Select the unit, for example SU001, in the **Outliner** or in the Matrix."
" Under its name and description, the **Inspector** shows the strip of its"
" media, with **Add images…** and **Link by name…**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **Add images…**. The window **Images for SU001** asks whether to "
"add **A picture…** or **A folder…**; choose one, then pick the file or "
"the folder in EMStudio's file picker. Each picture is identified by its "
"fingerprint (sha256): a picture whose bytes are already in the graph is "
"reused, not added twice."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To link many pictures at once, press **Link by name…**. EMStudio proposes"
" every picture whose file name opens with the code of a unit (for example"
" ``SU001_north.jpg`` for SU001). The window **Link by name** lists the "
"pairs, and the ambiguous names apart, which it never chooses for you. "
"Press **Link**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A picture that is already a node of the graph can also be dragged from "
"the viewer of the images onto a unit: onto its row in the **Outliner**, "
"or onto its strip in the **Inspector**. :kbd:`Esc` cancels the drag."
msgstr ""

msgid "Look at the media of a node"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Select a unit or a document. The strip shows the first pictures, the "
"frontispiece first, and the count, for example **33 media**, with the "
"number of models beside it when there are some."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Click the count or a picture. The viewer of the images opens in a Viewer "
"window, or under the **Inspector** when the workspace has none."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Choose how the tree on the left groups the pictures: **by folder**, **by "
"group** or **by role**. Each picture says whether it is **in the "
"document** that holds it or **not censused**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Click a picture to see it large; ‹ and › go to the previous and the next,"
" :kbd:`Esc` goes back to the grid."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The viewer stays on the node that opened it. Press ✕ (**Back to the "
"node's viewer**) to follow the selection again."
msgstr ""

msgid "Assign the photographs of a document to a unit"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A photographic campaign often covers many units. Open its pictures once, "
"and give each unit its own."
msgstr ""

msgid "Select the document of the campaign and open its pictures from the strip."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Select a unit in the **Outliner** or in the Matrix. The viewer stays on "
"the document, and the unit becomes the target of its commands. Tick "
"**only the photos of SU001** to see only the pictures the unit already "
"has."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Pick the pictures with a click while holding :kbd:`⌘` on macOS, or "
":kbd:`Ctrl` on Windows and Linux (:kbd:`⇧` works too). A second click "
"with the same key leaves a picture out again."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **Assign to SU001**: the button also shows how many pictures are "
"picked. **Remove from SU001** takes the picked pictures away from the "
"unit."
msgstr ""

msgid "Group the pictures and make documents of them"
msgstr ""

msgid "With some pictures picked in the viewer, the bar above the grid offers:"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Make a group…**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A logical group of the picked files, with a **Name** and a **Role** taken"
" from a vocabulary: photogrammetry, frontispiece, detail, vector drawing,"
" raster drawing, 3D model, orthophoto, archival documentation. **＋ new "
"role…** adds a role of the project. The group can be linked to the "
"selected unit, and the files stay where they are on the disk. Press "
"**Make the group**."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Give a name…**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **Name** and a **Description** for one picture, which becomes a node of"
" the graph. Press **Give the name**."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Census as a document…**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A new document, with the next free code of the series D.nn, that holds "
"the picked files (or the whole set shown). Press **Census**."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Place in the Space**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The picture, or the document, as a representation in the 3D scene, at the"
" 3D cursor. It is a placement made by you, and it can be moved (see :doc"
":`/how-to/space/place-objects`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each of these commands is one step of the history: ``Edit ▸ Undo`` takes "
"it back."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **Inspector** of a unit shows all the pictures that reach it, each "
"one once, and the viewer says by which road it comes: linked to the unit;"
" from a document that the unit cites; through the readings of a property,"
" that is a region traced on a picture (see :doc:`trace-regions-on-"
"images`); or because the picture illustrates a property of the unit. A "
"frontispiece comes first in every list. In the **Sheets** workspace, the "
"**Viewer & card** under the sheet shows the same pictures for the unit "
"chosen."
msgstr ""

msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`/tutorial/files-to-documents` and :doc:`/how-to/bring-in/porta-"
"dentro` bring files into the study as documents, with their date and "
"licence."
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`declare-provenance` says where each file comes from."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":ref:`reference-inspector` describes the sections of a document and of a "
"resource."
msgstr ""

